[01:04] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Diagram detailing Deno's approach: `deno desktop app.ts` serves UI to web view, backend runs in Deno, using WebView2 on Windows and WebKit on Mac/Linux.
AUDIO: 'Linux. So, use that. You point Deno desktop at a TypeScript file. And it serves your interface into that built-in webview while your back end runs in Deno.'

[01:17] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Diagram showing Deno's front-end/back-end binding in the same process, contrasting with Electron's IPC channel.
AUDIO: 'The two talk through a binding. You call a function on the front end, it runs on the back end in the same process. Electron needs a separate channel for that, called IPC. Deno skips it. And if you don\'t trust every operating'

